This project calls other people's software. What it calls, and on what terms:

Stata, pystata, mp-64.dll — StataCorp LLC
-----------------------------------------
The `pystata` engine (`bridge/stata_bridge.py`, `src/engines/pystata.mjs`) does
not ship Stata. It imports the `pystata` package and loads the shared library
(`mp-64.dll`, `se-64.dll` or `be-64.dll`) that come with a user's own Stata
installation, at `<Stata home>/utilities/pystata`. Stata, pystata and those
libraries are the property of StataCorp LLC and are governed by the Stata
licence; running this engine requires a licensed Stata installation.

Two interface facts worth stating plainly:

* `pystata`'s `config.init()`, `stata.run()` and `config.set_output_file()` are
  documented by StataCorp and are used exactly as documented.
* `StataSO_SetBreak` is an *undocumented* export of the same shared library. It
  is called in one place only, from the bridge's stdin thread, to interrupt a
  command that is already running — the same mechanism Stata's own automation
  interfaces use. Because the export is undocumented, treat it as best effort:
  if a future Stata release removes it, `stata_break` degrades to "no
  interruption" and nothing else changes. The engine test (`test/pystata.mjs`)
  fails loudly in that case.

stata-ai-skill — ZihaoVistonWang
-------------------------------
The `http` engine speaks the HTTP contract of
[stata-ai-skill](https://github.com/ZihaoVistonWang/Stata-AI-Skill) (MIT):
`GET /status`, `POST /execute`, `POST /break`, `POST /shutdown`, and the graph
capture behaviour. That project remains a supported way to run this server, and
its session manager, setup wizard and article search are not reimplemented here.
The engine adapter is deliberately thin so either side can evolve.

claude-code-my-workflow — Pedro Sant'Anna
-----------------------------------------
The workflow this project's skill pack encodes — numbered `analysis/NN-*.do`
stages, an `output/` tree, quality gates before a result is reported, a
`quality_reports/`-style audit trail — is modelled on
[pedrohcgs/claude-code-my-workflow](https://github.com/pedrohcgs/claude-code-my-workflow)
(MIT). The Stata tool surface here is different (structured results, lint,
signatures, background jobs), but the discipline is theirs.

Stata Tips, Fourth Edition (Nicholas J. Cox, ed.)
--------------------------------------------------
Three behaviours in this project were chosen because the *Stata Journal* "Stata
Tips" series documents them as silent failure modes rather than errors:

* `set varabbrev off` and exact variable names — Stata Tip 22,
  doi:10.1177/1536867X0500500314
* `datasignature` as a data fingerprint — Stata Tip 35,
  doi:10.1177/1536867X0600600312
* computing t, p and confidence intervals from `e(b)`/`e(V)` — Stata Tip 53,
  doi:10.1177/1536867X0800700408
* `missing()` taking an expression list, not a varlist — Stata Tip 86,
  doi:10.1177/1536867X1001000210
* `if` as a command testing one observation — Stata Tip 152,
  doi:10.1177/1536867X231175349

The `stata_lint` rules name the tip they come from in their `hint` text.

Model Context Protocol
----------------------
The protocol implementation (newline-delimited JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio) follows
the MCP specification, https://modelcontextprotocol.io. No MCP SDK code is
vendored; `src/server.mjs` is a small hand-written implementation.
